/458"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Free Rifle, Kneeling, 300 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "4 –  5 August 1900"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/205"} "Camp de Satory, Versailles"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/51512"} "30 from 6 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "300 metres. 40 shots kneeling. 400 possible."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The target in this event was 1 metre in diameter with 10 scoring rings, with a black aiming mark of 60 cm in diameter. Shooters shot 40 shots from 300 metres in a kneeling position, for a possible 400 points. This event was a part of the 3-position free rifle event, which was shot concurrently for " [:a {:href "/results/51510"} "individuals"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/51516"} "teams"] " and, like the other two elements, prone and standing, it counted as a separate event, with medals being awarded accordingly. It was the only time at the Olympics that this happened."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For the first and only time, the World Championships were held concurrently with the Olympics, and the Olympic Champions also became World Champions. It was the fourth staging of the World Championships, having previously been held in Lyon (France) in 1897, Turin (Italy) in 1898, and Loosduinen in 1899 (which, since 1923, has been a district of Den Haag, in the Netherlands)."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/44109"} "Konrad Stäheli"] " of Switzerland had won the last two World Championships and was favorite to defend his title in Paris. The 1899 silver and bronze medalists, " [:a {:href "/athletes/44071"} "Emil Kellenberger"] " (SUI) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/43434"} "Henrik Sillem"] " (NED), who was also the 1897 bronze medalist, were also competing, as were the 1898 French silver and bronze medalists " [:a {:href "/athletes/42206"} "Achille Paroche"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/42199"} "Léon Moreaux"] ". All the World Championship medalists since 1897 appeared in Paris, with the exception of the 1897 gold and silver medalists Frank Jullien and Alcide Hirschy, both from Switzerland."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Konrad Stäheli was in a class of his own and won the first Olympic title and, with it, his third World championship title in this event. He would go on to add another seven World titles to his collection, in 1901-02 and 1910-1914, for a total of ten titles. He won four medals, including three golds in 1900, but, despite his long career, appeared in just one more Olympics, at the 1906 Intercalated Games, when he added a " [:a {:href "/results/51528"} "gold"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/51525"} "silver medal"] " to his tally."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/44109"} "Konrad Stäheli"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} "324"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/41796"} "Anders Peter Nielsen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/DEN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/DEN.png"}] "DEN"]] [:td {} "314"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/44071"} "Emil Kellenberger"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} "314"] [:td {} [:sp